--   1. legacy_codes gains the source `own`: the household's own codes, typed, numbered or
--      imported, resolved like any legacy code (scan, search, the jump box). Unique per location
--      by the primary key (source `own`, collection ''); the server also refuses a code the
--      location has under another source. `legacy_codes_any_code_idx` answers a code looked up
--      whatever its source.
--   2. own_code_settings (numbering and the format rule; read by members, written by owners and
--      admins) and own_code_counters (the numbering's counters: read by members, moved only
--      through the door).
--   3. kept.next_own_code(location): the next numbered code, the counter row incremented under
--      its lock in the caller's transaction, skipping a code the location already has. A number
--      is never taken twice, even after its code is deleted.
--   4. A new confirmed thing gets a numbered code when the location numbers them
--      (kept.number_new_thing, a constraint trigger AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OF review_state,
--      deferred to the end of the transaction: a draft is numbered when it is confirmed, and a
--      thing that has an own code by then gets none: an import row's own code, a place turned
--      into a box keeping the place's). Deferred, the counter's lock is held only while the
--      transaction commits.
--   5. Removals reach phones: sync_tombstones gains entity_key, the text key of a removed code
--      or legacy code (`legacyCodeKey()`: location:source:collection:code), with
--      entity_id = md5(entity_key)::uuid so the primary key still holds one row per key.
--      A deleted legacy code writes its tombstone (kept.tombstone_legacy_code), unless its
--      location is going too; one that arrives clears it (kept.clear_code_tombstone_on_arrival).
